.TH "DEDB RUN" "1" "2026-01-01" "0.0.18" "dedb run Manual"
.SH NAME
dedb\-run \- Run a game in DOSBox or DOSEMU2.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dedb run
[OPTIONS] GAME [EMULATOR_ARGS]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run a game in DOSBox or DOSEMU2.
.PP
GAME is specified as <scheme>:<id> or -b <scheme> <identifier>,
or just <identifier> for downloaded games.
.PP
An emulator must be specified with --dosbox or --dosemu.
.PP
Arguments after `--` go straight to the emulator.
.PP
--cmdline shows the emulator commandline instead of running it.
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-dosbox\fP
Run in DOSBox.
.TP
\fB\-\-dosemu\fP
Run in DOSEMU2.
.TP
\fB\-\-profile\fP TEXT
Launch profile (gog:// only). Same as gog://<id>?profile=<slug>.
.TP
\fB\-b,\fP \-\-backend SCHEME
Read GAME as a bare id for this backend, rather than a <scheme>://<id> URL.
.TP
\fB\-\-redownload\fP
Re-download and re-extract even if already present.
.TP
\fB\-r,\fP \-\-refreshmetadata
Re-fetch cached backend metadata instead of using the cached copy.
.TP
\fB\-\-keep\fP
Keep the installer/archive after extracting.
.TP
\fB\-v,\fP \-\-verbose
Print the command line before launching.
.TP
\fB\-\-cmdline\fP
Print the command --dosbox/--dosemu would run and stop (needs the game downloaded; writes nothing).
